# Keep the build context small and, more importantly, keep secrets out of it.
#
# .env is the one that matters: it is gitignored, so it never reaches a clone,
# but a Docker build sends the whole working directory to the daemon and COPY
# would happily bake it into a layer. Deployed configuration arrives as
# environment variables at run time, never in the image.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Build output. target/ is rebuilt inside the builder stage; copying a local
# one in would both bloat the context and risk shipping a stale jar.
target/
**/target/

.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.idea/
*.iml
.vscode/
.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar

# Local-only infrastructure and tooling. None of it is part of the image:
# compose runs the stand-ins a developer needs, and the deployed instance
# talks to the managed services instead.
docker-compose.yml
docker/
requests/
http-client.env.json
*.md
!README.md
